1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:05,411
This last video covers four small topics that each show up as one or two questions,

2
00:00:05,411 --> 00:00:07,497
then a playbook for the exam itself.

3
00:00:09,100 --> 00:00:11,671
K-means groups points into K clusters.

4
00:00:11,671 --> 00:00:13,160
Pick K random centers.

5
00:00:13,160 --> 00:00:15,867
Assign each point to its nearest center.

6
00:00:15,867 --> 00:00:18,776
Move each center to the mean of its points.

7
00:00:18,776 --> 00:00:20,738
Repeat until nothing changes.

8
00:00:21,520 --> 00:00:22,422
Two exam facts.

9
00:00:22,422 --> 00:00:24,347
You choose K, not the algorithm.

10
00:00:24,347 --> 00:00:27,654
To compare K from 5 to 10, run it once per K: six runs.

11
00:00:27,654 --> 00:00:30,481
Then compare the within-cluster sum of squares,

12
00:00:30,481 --> 00:00:32,826
and how interpretable the clusters are.

13
00:00:32,826 --> 00:00:37,758
And because the starting centers are random, two runs can give different clusters.

14
00:00:37,758 --> 00:00:39,923
That's true on practice midterm two.

15
00:00:39,923 --> 00:00:44,734
In practice, run several starts and keep the best, and scale the features first,

16
00:00:44,734 --> 00:00:46,659
since K-means runs on distances.

17
00:00:48,267 --> 00:00:51,472
For time series, match the plot to the model terms.

18
00:00:51,472 --> 00:00:54,741
A steady drift calls for a trend term, beta times t.

19
00:00:54,741 --> 00:00:59,518
A pattern that repeats each year or week calls for month or weekday dummies.

20
00:00:59,518 --> 00:01:04,484
And if each day looks like the day before, add the previous value as a feature.

21
00:01:05,253 --> 00:01:10,027
Practice midterm one's bike rentals climb over two years, peak every summer,

22
00:01:10,027 --> 00:01:13,796
and track the previous day, so the answer uses all three: D.

23
00:01:14,553 --> 00:01:20,093
The autoregressive model, AR(1), is X t equals beta zero plus beta one times X t

24
00:01:20,093 --> 00:01:21,617
minus one, plus noise.

25
00:01:21,617 --> 00:01:25,495
Practice midterm two's version had a time trend instead,

26
00:01:25,495 --> 00:01:30,688
and the previous value with no coefficient, so it's not an AR model: false.

27
00:01:30,688 --> 00:01:35,189
And always split training and test data by date, never at random.

28
00:01:36,800 --> 00:01:41,101
Bag of words turns a document into word counts and ignores the order.

29
00:01:41,101 --> 00:01:45,527
So writing a sentence twice doubles its counts, and the vectors differ.

30
00:01:45,527 --> 00:01:47,958
That's false on practice midterm three.

31
00:01:48,720 --> 00:01:54,116
Zipf's law says the r-th most common word appears about one over r as often as the

32
00:01:54,116 --> 00:01:55,169
most common one.

33
00:01:55,169 --> 00:02:00,631
That's a heavy tail: rare words, added together, make up a large share of the text.

34
00:02:00,631 --> 00:02:05,238
So practice midterm two's claim of a light, exponential tail is false.

35
00:02:06,833 --> 00:02:11,941
Practice midterm one's last question asks for two features built from the booking

36
00:02:11,941 --> 00:02:15,345
and trip timestamps, with at least one that uses both.

37
00:02:15,345 --> 00:02:20,516
From one timestamp: day of week, hour, month, or a weekend flag, coded as dummies.

38
00:02:20,516 --> 00:02:24,740
From both: the lead time, trip time minus booking time, in minutes.

39
00:02:25,520 --> 00:02:28,629
And only build features you'd know at prediction time.

40
00:02:28,629 --> 00:02:31,681
Anything recorded after the outcome leaks the answer.

41
00:02:33,267 --> 00:02:34,945
Finally, the exam playbook.

42
00:02:34,945 --> 00:02:37,120
Most questions fall into six types.

43
00:02:37,120 --> 00:02:41,718
Statements with always, never, or guaranteed: hunt for one counterexample,

44
00:02:41,718 --> 00:02:43,893
which is enough to make them false.

45
00:02:43,893 --> 00:02:48,679
For the most definitive statement, find the tightest range that's guaranteed.

46
00:02:48,679 --> 00:02:53,775
And for 'can a new feature help': a linear combination of existing features can't,

47
00:02:53,775 --> 00:02:55,639
but a nonlinear transform can.

48
00:02:56,420 --> 00:03:01,098
For 'is there enough information': trace every branch you can't rule out,

49
00:03:01,098 --> 00:03:04,366
and remember that a prediction isn't a probability.

50
00:03:04,366 --> 00:03:09,108
For computations from output, write the expression first, then the number.

51
00:03:09,108 --> 00:03:13,594
And for justification questions, quote the evidence: a p-value, a VIF,

52
00:03:13,594 --> 00:03:15,196
a cross-validation score.

53
00:03:15,953 --> 00:03:19,246
One tiebreaker for true or false when you're stuck.

54
00:03:19,246 --> 00:03:24,541
In these three exams, always, impossible and guaranteed claims were usually false,

55
00:03:24,541 --> 00:03:28,285
and 'it is possible that' was true both times it appeared.

56
00:03:28,285 --> 00:03:30,287
The exceptions were math facts.

57
00:03:30,287 --> 00:03:32,159
Use it only as a last resort.

58
00:03:33,767 --> 00:03:35,191
That's the whole series.

59
00:03:35,191 --> 00:03:39,877
Next, work the practice problems in the study guide under a timer; they're your

60
00:03:39,877 --> 00:03:40,530
fresh test.

61
00:03:40,530 --> 00:03:43,378
Drill the multiple choice answers as flashcards,

62
00:03:43,378 --> 00:03:45,514
and build your note sheet as you go.

63
00:03:45,514 --> 00:03:48,895
Then take one practice midterm with just your note sheet.

64
00:03:48,895 --> 00:03:50,378
Good luck on the midterm.
